# OWNER_DECISIONS.md

> Deliberate decisions are not defects. Everything recorded here is off,
> deferred or retired because somebody decided it, and the decision stands
> until its lifting condition fires.

---

## 1. Three states, not two

A surface that is not doing its job has three possible explanations, and only
two of them are problems:

| State | What it means | Who acts |
|---|---|---|
| **working** | It does what it claims | nobody |
| **broken** | It claims to work and does not | the crew, now |
| **off on purpose** | It is not running, and that is the decision | nobody, until the lifting condition fires |

A registry that only knows the first two forces every reader to guess, and the
guess is always "broken" - which is how a deliberate deferral gets re-filed as
a finding every single review round, and eventually gets "fixed" by somebody
who was not in the room.

---

## 2. Entry schema

```
id            stable, never reused: OD-<NNN>
subject       the EXACT thing this covers - one id, one path, one flag, one
              check name. Never a wildcard, never a category.
state         deferred | disabled | retired
decided       <YYYY-MM-DD>, by the owner
reason        why, in one sentence somebody can disagree with
lifts-when    the condition that ends this entry - REQUIRED
evidence      where the subject shows up when it does (so it can still be
              reported, see rule 3.3)
```

**Both `reason` and `lifts-when` are required.** An entry missing either is
**ignored loudly**: it is reported as a defect in this registry and it does NOT
suppress anything. A silent partial entry would be the strongest thing in the
repository - a standing suppression nobody has to justify.

---

## 3. The rules that keep this from being a suppression list

**3.1 One id per entry, never a wildcard.** A decision covers the thing that
was decided. "All warnings from <SUBSYSTEM>" is a decision nobody made about
findings nobody has seen yet, and it hides the next real one.

**3.2 A standing entry is never re-raised.** Not as a finding, not as a
proactive issue, not as a footnote, not as a "worth revisiting" aside. Re-raising
it is the cost this file exists to remove, and a footnote is a re-raise wearing
a smaller font.

**3.3 It is still REPORTED, with evidence, whenever its subject appears.** A
standing decision moves an item off the **proactive** list; it does not delete
the observation. The finding becomes a report-with-evidence citing the entry id
- "observed, covered by OD-<NNN>, lifts when <CONDITION>" - so the owner can
see the cost of their own decision accruing and change their mind with data.
Suppressing the observation itself would make the decision unfalsifiable.

**3.4 A decision is never applied to an UNKNOWN reading.** An entry covers a
KNOWN state of a subject. When the instrument could not look - unreachable,
timed out, not installed, unparseable - the answer is `unknown`, which is a
fact about the instrument and not about the subject the owner decided about.
Applying a decision to an unknown converts a broken probe into a sanctioned
silence, which is the worst of the three states wearing the best one's name.

**3.5 No other surface may promise the deferred behavior.** If paging is off by
decision, nothing may say "you will be paged". Every promise of a behavior that
is currently off is a defect, and the sweep for those promises is part of
landing the entry, not a follow-up.

**3.6 The lifting condition is real work, not a date.** "Revisit in Q3" lifts
nothing; it just moves the argument. A lifting condition names an observable
event: a dependency reaching a version, a rewrite landing, a measured rate
falling below a threshold, an owner decision recorded as its own ruling.

**3.7 Retiring an entry is a change, not a deletion.** When the condition
fires, the entry moves to section 5 with the date it lifted, and the subject
returns to the proactive list **in the same change** - otherwise the decision
outlives its own reason and nobody notices.

