## 5. Lifted and retired

Kept, not deleted: the history is what stops the same decision being made,
reversed and re-made by three sessions that never met.

| id | subject | lifted | what fired the condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| <OD_NNN> | <EXACT_SUBJECT_ID> | <YYYY_MM_DD> | <WHAT_HAPPENED> |

---

## 6. Who may write here

Only the owner decides. A session may **draft** a proposed entry and put it on
the human queue with its reason and a candidate lifting condition; it may not
add one, widen one, or extend one to a neighbouring subject. A crew that can
write its own suppressions has no findings.

---

## 7. The irreversible-surfaces register

Numbered 7 deliberately: it is the other register the owner keeps, and it lives
beside the decisions rather than in a file of its own so there is one place to
look before touching something that does not come back.

**Rigour scales with irreversibility.** Most work is cheap to undo - a branch, a
change, a revert - and that reversibility is what makes speed safe. The
surfaces below are the exceptions, and the standard's weight belongs there
(`docs/ENGINEERING_STANDARD.md`, Mindset; the `<IRREVERSIBLE>` binding points
here).

**The membership test is one question: can a revert restore it?** State that is
not in version control does not come back with the code - accumulated learning,
production data, a message already sent, a payment taken, a credential
published, a header a client has already pinned, a record a third party now
holds.

| id | surface | why a revert does not restore it | the probe that reports after a change |
|---|---|---|---|
| IR-001 | <EXACT_SURFACE> | <WHAT_PERSISTS_OUTSIDE_VERSION_CONTROL> | <COMMAND_OR_CHECK_AGAINST_THE_REAL_SYSTEM> |
| IR-002 | <EXACT_SURFACE> | <WHAT_PERSISTS_OUTSIDE_VERSION_CONTROL> | <COMMAND_OR_CHECK_AGAINST_THE_REAL_SYSTEM> |

Four rules govern the register:

**7.1 Before touching one, slow down deliberately** and say what cannot be
undone. That sentence is written in the change, not felt.

**7.2 After touching one, the evidence is a probe against the real system** -
never the fact that a file changed, and never a green suite. **A green suite
describes a runner; only the probe describes the world.** The probe command is
part of the register, so nobody has to invent one under pressure.

**7.3 An empty register is not an exemption - it is the first gap to close.**
Every repository has at least one of these; a register with no rows means
nobody has looked yet, and that is a finding about this file.

**7.4 A surface here is not automatically off-limits.** This is not a
suppression list and it is not a ban. It raises the evidence bar for changes
that touch the surface, and the ship checklist reads it: a change touching a
registered surface is not done until its probe has reported.
